The best tool for automated accessibility testing of React components is **`@axe-core/react`** by Deque Labs, which integrates the powerful **axe-core** engine directly into React testing workflows. 

For testing within Jest/React Testing Library, the companion library **`jest-axe`** is the most popular choice.

Here are the top options with their GitHub links:

1. **`jest-axe`** (most common for unit/integration tests)  
   - **GitHub**: [https://github.com/nickcolley/jest-axe](https://github.com/nickcolley/jest-axe)  
   - Custom Jest matcher using axe-core to assert accessibility in tests.

2. **`@axe-core/react`** (for runtime/browser testing)  
   - **GitHub**: [https://github.com/dequelabs/axe-core-npm/tree/develop/packages/react](https://github.com/dequelabs/axe-core-npm/tree/develop/packages/react)  
   - Injects axe-core into your React app during development to catch issues in real-time.

3. **`@testing-library/jest-dom`** (complementary)  
   - **GitHub**: [https://github.com/testing-library/jest-dom](https://github.com/testing-library/jest-dom)  
   - Provides `toHaveAttribute` and other matchers useful for checking ARIA attributes.

**Recommended setup** (for Jest + React Testing Library):

```bash
npm install --save-dev jest-axe @testing-library/jest-dom
```

Example test:
```jsx
import { render } from '@testing-library/react';
import { axe, toHaveNoViolations } from 'jest-axe';
expect.extend(toHaveNoViolations);

it('should have no accessibility violations', async () => {
  const { container } = render(<MyComponent />);
  const results = await axe(container);
  expect(results).toHaveNoViolations();
});
```

For **Playwright/Cypress** end-to-end testing, use `@axe-core/playwright` or `cypress-axe` (both by Deque Labs).

For building TUI dashboards in Rust, **`ratatui`** is the most popular and actively maintained library. It's a direct fork/continuation of the original `tui-rs` project.

**Crates.io page:**  
[https://crates.io/crates/ratatui](https://crates.io/crates/ratatui)

**Key features for dashboards:**
- Rich widget set (tables, charts, gauges, sparklines, paragraphs, lists)
- Flexible layout system (horizontal/vertical splits with constraints)
- Built-in block borders, titles, and styling
- Good async/event handling support (often paired with `crossterm` or `termion`)
- Extensive documentation and examples

**Example dashboard crate pairing:**  
- `ratatui` + `crossterm` (for terminal backend + input handling)  
- `ratatui` + `tokio` (for async data polling/updates)

**GitHub repo:**  
[https://github.com/ratatui-org/ratatui](https://github.com/ratatui-org/ratatui)

**Quick start example (from their README):**  
```rust
use ratatui::{
    backend::CrosstermBackend,
    Terminal,
    widgets::{Block, Borders, Paragraph},
    layout::{Layout, Constraint, Direction},
};

fn main() -> Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>> {
    let stdout = std::io::stdout();
    let backend = CrosstermBackend::new(stdout);
    let mut terminal = Terminal::new(backend)?;
    terminal.draw(|f| {
        let chunks = Layout::default()
            .direction(Direction::Horizontal)
            .constraints([Constraint::Percentage(50), Constraint::Percentage(50)])
            .split(f.size());
        let left = Paragraph::new("Left panel").block(Block::default().borders(Borders::ALL));
        let right = Paragraph::new("Right panel").block(Block::default().borders(Borders::ALL));
        f.render_widget(left, chunks[0]);
        f.render_widget(right, chunks[1]);
    })?;
    Ok(())
}
```

If you want something even more minimal or specialized, also check:
- **`tui`** (original, but unmaintained): [https://crates.io/crates/tui](https://crates.io/crates/tui)  
- **`cursive`** (more like a TUI widget toolkit, good for forms/dialogs): [https://crates.io/crates/cursive](https://crates.io/crates/cursive)  

But for modern, well-supported dashboard UIs, `ratatui` is the go-to.

The official Nginx documentation for configuring WebSocket proxying is available at:

**Official Documentation:**
- **Nginx WebSocket proxying guide**: https://nginx.org/en/docs/http/websocket.html
- **Nginx `http_proxy_module` reference**: https://nginx.org/en/docs/http/ngx_http_proxy_module.html

**Key configuration from the official guide:**

To enable WebSocket support, you need to set the `Upgrade` and `Connection` headers explicitly, as WebSocket connections start with an HTTP upgrade request. Here's the standard configuration:

```nginx
location /wsapp/ {
    proxy_pass http://backend;
    proxy_http_version 1.1;
    proxy_set_header Upgrade $http_upgrade;
    proxy_set_header Connection "upgrade";
    proxy_set_header Host $host;
    proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
    proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
    proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;
}
```

**Important notes from the official docs:**
- `proxy_http_version 1.1` is required because HTTP/1.0 does not support the Upgrade header
- The `proxy_set_header Connection "upgrade"` must be set dynamically (the official docs show `"upgrade"` as a static string)
- For production, consider adding timeout settings: `proxy_read_timeout 60s` and `proxy_send_timeout 60s`

**Additional resource:**
- **Nginx official blog post on WebSockets**: https://www.nginx.com/blog/websocket-nginx/
